'''aught<sup>1</sup>''' (ât), ''n.'' or ''pron.'' [In two forms: (1) ''aught'', < ME. ''aught'', ''auȝht'', ''auht'', ''aght'', ''aȝt'', ''aht'', < AS. ''awiht'', ''awuht'', with vowel shortened from orig. long, ''āwiht''; (2) ''ought'', < ME. ''ought'', ''ouht'', ''oght'', ''oȝt'', ''oht'', < AS. ''āwiht'', ''āwuht'', contr. ''āht'', with labialized vowel, ''ōwiht'', ''ōwuht'' (= OS. ''eowiht'' = OFries. ''āwet'', ''āet'' = D. ''iets'' = OHG. ''eowiht'', ''iowiht'', ''iewiht'', MHG. ''ieht'', ''iht'', ''iewet'', ''iet''), < ''ā'', ever, in comp. a generalizing prefix, + ''wiht'', wight, whit, thing: lit. ‘ever a whit’: see ''ay''<sup>1</sup> and ''whit'', ''wight'', and cf. the negative ''naught'', ''nought'', ‘never a whit.’ There is no essential difference between the two spellings ''aught'' and ''ought''; the former is now preferred.] Anything whatever; any part: used in interrogative, negative, and conditional sentences.
{{smaller block|Is there ''aught'' else, my friends, I can do for you?
{{right|''Addison'', Cato, iv. 4.}}
Unfaith in ''aught'' is want of faith in all.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien.}}}}

'''aught<sup>1</sup>†''' (ât), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''aught'', etc.; prop. acc. of the noun.] In any respect; in any way; at all; by any chance.
{{smaller block|Can he ''aught'' telle a mery tale or tweye?
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Canon’s Yeoman’s Tale, l. 44.}}
<poem>Thereon mused he
If that the childes moder were ''aught'' she
That was his wyf.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Man of Law’s Tale, l. 936.}}}}

