'''augment''' (âg-ment′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''augmenten'', < OF. ''augmenter'', earlier ''aumenter'' = Sp. ''aumentar'' = Pg. ''augmentar'' = It. ''aumentare'', < LL. ''augmentare'', increase, < L. ''augmentum'', an increase: see ''augment'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To increase; enlarge in size or extent; swell: as, to ''augment'' an army by reinforcement; impatience ''augments'' an evil.
{{smaller block|<poem>Be it your care
To ''augment'' your heap of wealth.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Elder Brother, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Though fortune change, his constant spouse remains:
''Augments'' his joys or mitigates his pains.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', January and May, l. 42.}}}}
{{smaller block|The general distress did but ''augment'' the piety and confirm the fortitude of the colonists.
{{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 284.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''gram.'', to add an augment to.
{{smaller block|Most [Greek] verbs beginning with a consonant ''augment'' the imperfect and aorist by prefixing ε.
{{right|''Goodwin'', Greek Gram., § 101.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''her.'', to make an honorable addition to, as a coat of arms.
{{smaller block|Henry VIII. granted to the earl of Surrey to ''augment'' his arms with a demi-lion, gules, pierced through the}}

