{{smaller|'''Augmented interval.''' See ''interval.''—'''Augmented surface,''' a term first used by Rankine to denote an immersed or wetted surface sufficiently greater than the actual surface of a vessel to give, when substituted for the actual quantity in estimations of the speed of a vessel, results which conform to the actual performance.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become greater in size, amount, degree, etc.; increase; grow larger.

{{smaller block|The winds redouble and the streams ''augment.''
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Virgil’s Georgics, i. 466.}}
Her fears ''augmented'' as her comforts fled.
{{right|''Crabbe'', Tales of the Hall.}}}}

'''augmentable''' (âg-men′ta̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''augment'' + ''-able.''] Capable of being augmented or increased.

'''augmentation''' (âg-men-tā′sho̤n), ''n.'' [< ML. ''augmentatio(n-)'', < LL. ''augmentare'', pp. ''augmentatus'', augment: see ''augment, v.''] '''1.''' The act of increasing or making larger by addition, expansion, or dilatation; the act of adding to or enlarging; the state or condition of being made larger.

{{smaller block|Bacon, holding that this method was insufficient and futile for the ''augmentation'' of real and useful knowledge, published his Novum Organon.
{{right|''Whewell'', Nov. Org. Renovatum, Pref.}}}}

'''2.''' That by which anything is augmented; an addition: as, the ''augmentation'' amounted to $500 a year.

{{smaller block|He does smile his face into more lines than are in the new map with the ''augmentation'' of the Indies.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 2.}}}}

Specifically—'''3.''' In ''music'', where much repetition and imitation of themes is required, the

